  Measurement stance: the subject is WHICH UDP broadcasts the DUT relays, so it
  cannot be graded on a ping or on `show running-config` (STANDING-ORDERS §7).
  Every leg sends the same crafted frames from eth3 and counts what arrives on
  eth4, and every leg carries two controls in the same capture: a UDP flow on port
  7777, never in `ip forward-protocol`, which must NEVER be relayed and which stops
  "a broadcast arrived" being mistaken for "the helper relayed it"; and a routed
  ICMP unicast 10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.65, which must ALWAYS arrive and which
  proves the DUT was routing between the two segments and the eth4 instrument was
  alive.

STEP 0 -- baseline: prove the commands exist, read-only                           done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: tools/qmark.py, no trailing carriage return, so nothing is executed. The
  driver's self-test ran first: "self-test OK: 'show clock ?' then Ctrl-U then CR
  executed nothing".

awplus(config)#ip forward-protocol  ?
  udp  User Datagram Protocol(UDP)
awplus(config)#ip forward-protocol udp  ?
  PORT  UDP Port number
awplus(config-if)#ip helper-address  ?
  A.B.C.D  Forwarding destination address for IP Helper

  => STEP 0 done: both commands exist, nothing here is UNSUPPORTED, and the exact
     shape of the CLI is now on record. `ip forward-protocol` accepts only `udp`
     -- there is no `nd` or other protocol form -- and `ip forward-protocol udp`
     accepts only a NUMERIC port: there are no named service keywords on this
     platform (no `tftp`, `domain`, `netbios-ns`, `time`). `ip helper-address`
     takes exactly one bare `A.B.C.D`, with no interface form and no VRF keyword.
     A case written against the Cisco-style named forms would fail at its first
     command, which is why the probe comes before the measurement.

