    x5     ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.40     -> 10.38.22.65     [ours]   [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f]
                                           <-- :7777 is ABSENT. 15 frames did
                                               arrive in this capture, so its
                                               zero is a measurement.

  Reading the relayed frames field by field, against the ingress capture above:
    - the all-ones broadcast 255.255.255.255:69 was relayed 5/5, and the ingress
      subnet's DIRECTED broadcast 10.38.22.63:37 was relayed 5/5. The guide says
      only "UDP broadcasts received on the interface" without distinguishing the
      two forms; measured, both are relayed;
    - each is rewritten to the helper address 10.38.22.65 and sent as a UNICAST,
      L2 to the host's own MAC, with the DUT's MAC as L2 source;
    - the IP SOURCE is still 10.38.22.40, the original sender, and BOTH UDP ports
      are unchanged. Only the IP destination and the Ethernet header are rewritten:
      there is no giaddr-style rewrite and no relay-agent option. IP Helper is a
      plain forwarder, which is the opposite of `ip dhcp-relay` on the same box, so
      a server behind the helper sees the client's real address and a tester
      looking for a giaddr rewrite will not find one.

  => STEP 2 PASS: with a port in `ip forward-protocol udp` and a helper address on
     the ingress interface, UDP broadcasts of that port cross into the far subnet
     as unicasts to the helper address, in both broadcast forms, while an
     identically-shaped broadcast on an unhelped port does not cross at all. That
     is the guide's core claim, with its own counter-example in the same capture.

